        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          OBJECTIVES:<br>
The principal objective of this NSBRI Smart Medical Systems Team project is to develop an image-guided ultrasound therapy system for mission critical care. In long-term space flight missions, a number of medical situations could develop that if not adequately addressed would result in mission failure. For example, although gravity is significantly reduced in space, inertia is not, and the collision of an astronaut with a heavy object could result in blunt internal trauma and it’s often associated internal bleeding. Investigators have demonstrated that a device that produces High Intensity Focused Ultrasound (HIFU) can be combined with a device that provides ultrasound imaging to produce a duplex system that can both image a particular condition of interest and provide therapy to relieve the condition. Image-Guided Therapy provides enormous potential for the treatment of a variety of medical conditions. 
<P>
Specific Aim 1:  To develop a combined ultrasound guidance and therapy system. This first-generation system should have the following components: (A) Laptop computer control, (B) Software control, (C) Compatibility with commercial ultrasound imaging systems, (D) Single element resonant transducer, and (E) Dynamic depth focusing. 
<P>
Specific Aim 2:  To perform studies on the combined system that would lead to optimal performance parameters. Among the studies to be performed are the following: (A) Biological effects, tissue necrosis, acoustic hemostasis, (B) Image quality for diagnosis, (C) Targeting and monitoring capabilities, (D) Acoustic focusing and power requirements, and (E) Thermal focusing limitations. 
<P>
Specific Aim 3:  Utilizing the results of SAs 1 and 2, to develop an integrated ultrasound guidance and therapy system. 
<P>
APPROACH:<br>
The methodology is embedded in the results below.
<p>
RESULTS: <br>
 The 3 Specific Aims have been met. Aim 1 was completed and reported in year 1. The ability to use ultrasound to guide HIFU is the foundation of the integrated system developed in Aim 3. Aim 2 is the science to make the system clinically useful. The investigator team continues to lead the world in the field of acoustic hemostasis. The NSBRI project has leveraged other funding to use HIFU to stop bleeding. Investigators have found they can break kidney stones with HIFU. In particular the NSBRI research has led to the two most powerful ways to enhance or optimize tissue heating – short, strong pulses to exploit nonlinear wave propagation and dual-frequency mixing to exploit cavitation bubbles. Aim 3 has led to the most portable and versatile guided HIFU system they know of. They have developed (A) Integrated imaging and HIFU therapy transducers, (B) Cavitation feedback, (C) Attenuation/thermal feedback to localize treatment site, (D) Perfusion and back-scatter imaging for treatment localization, and (E) Software control and friendly user interface. 
<p>
Investigators found they could integrate the three key findings of year 2 into a useful part of their system and expand on those findings. The findings were not solely academic discoveries but became enabling technologies. One, the protocol of using short high-amplitude pulses has been integrated in the system. They showed that doubling the acoustic amplitude of the source cuts the treatment time by more than half. In year three, they utilized this technology and defined the mechanisms underlying the effect. Two, their lesion identification algorithm has been improved to even measure temperature of HIFU heated tissue before a lesion even forms. This enables safe and accurate targeting. Tests are underway of the system connected to the HDI-1000 imager with this imaging capability. Three, the patenting and licensing process continues for our circuit to permit real-time synchronization of HIFU therapy with an arbitrary ultrasound imager. Commercial partners may soon be using the technology to develop even smaller and more powerful systems than we have developed. Second, they developed a complete integrated guided system within a suitcase. The system could be synchronized with an arbitrary ultrasound imager, such as the Philips HDI-5000 on the ISS. Or they added in year three to use a simplified Doppler imaging system to locate the HIFU focus on a major bleed by visual or audio guidance. This is an uncomplicated user-friendly device to rapidly and accurately treat a mission critical bleed. Third, they discovered using two frequencies instead of one HIFU frequency they could accelerate hemostasis times 25%. They completed a careful study numerically and experimentally in vivo and in vitro that showed how two frequencies can increase cavitation, enhance heat deposition and accelerate hemostasis. 
<p>
The components now weighs less than 7 kg, down from more than 30 kg in year 1, and 40 kg at the start of the project. They are packaged in a single chassis (a suitcase) and operate with the Philips HDI-5000 ultrasound imager on the ISS or whatever imager is chosen for long-duration missions (i.e., a Terason or a Sonosite imager such as used in the SMS project lead by Dr. J. Thomas). Doppler makes a simple rapid method stop major bleeds without even the use of the ultrasound imager. They have established a protocol to sweep short-duration, high-amplitude pulses produced by the dynamic focusing transducer to treat large areas rapidly without complications from cavitation. This includes sealing exposed lung from blood and air leakage. These findings expand trauma care capabilities, improve therapy efficiency, and minimize the power draw required by the device. When two frequencies are mixed, the enhancement is even greater. Bleeds stop 25% quicker. In addition, since heating and greater, higher volume bleeds can likely be stopped. Lastly, since the heated region is broader, likely larger vessels can be treated than were previously possible.
<p>
        </div>

